TRD – Action 1: Google sign‑up / sign‑in
A. Google Cloud Console setup (for auth)
Create project

Go to Google Cloud Console → create/select a project.

Enable “Google Identity Services / OAuth”

Open “APIs & Services → Credentials”.

Configure OAuth consent screen

In “APIs & Services → OAuth consent screen”:

User type: “External”.

App name, support email, etc.

For Action 1 you only need basic OpenID scopes: openid, email, profile.

Add yourself as test user while developing.

Create OAuth 2.0 client (Web application)

In “Credentials → Create credentials → OAuth client ID → Application type: Web application”.

Authorized redirect URIs (examples):

Local: http://localhost:3000/api/auth/google/callback

Prod: https://your-domain.com/api/auth/google/callback

Save Client ID and Client Secret – use only on backend.

B. Auth flow design (high‑level)
Use the standard OAuth 2.0 “web server application” flow: browser is redirected to Google, user selects account, Google sends code back to your backend, backend exchanges code for tokens.

Frontend shows the “Continue with Google / Sign in with Google” button.

Backend is responsible for:

Redirecting to Google with proper query params.

Handling callback.

Exchanging auth code → tokens.

Creating/finding user.

Issuing your own JWT.

C. Endpoints (suggested)
Backend (NestJS/Express style):

GET /auth/google

Query: mode=signup or mode=signin.

Logic:

Build Google auth URL with:

client_id (from console)

redirect_uri (/auth/google/callback)

response_type=code

scope=openid email profile

access_type=offline (gives refresh token if you ever need it later)

state (random string, also store in session/DB to protect against CSRF)

Redirect browser to this URL.

GET /auth/google/callback

Receives code and state.

Steps:

Validate state matches what you stored.

Exchange code for tokens via Google OAuth endpoint or googleapis npm client.

You get: access_token, id_token, (maybe refresh_token).

Verify id_token:

Validate signature and audience with Google public keys (library does this for you).

Extract claims: sub (Google user id), email, email_verified, name, picture.

Map to your user:

If mode=signup:

If user with this email already exists:

Option A: Treat as login and just link Google to that user.

Option B: Return “Email already registered. Please sign in.”

If not exists: create user row:

id, email, name, avatar_url, google_sub, auth_provider='google' (or 'local+google').

If mode=signin:

Lookup by email or by google_sub.

If user not found → return error “Account does not exist”.

Issue your own JWT (same as your normal login flow).

Redirect back to frontend with JWT (e.g., via cookie or URL fragment).

D. DB changes (minimal)
Add to users table:

google_sub (string, nullable) – Google’s stable user id.

auth_provider (enum: local, google, local+google).

Optionally separate user_providers table if you want many providers later.